! `bismuthene-soc-small.in` with fixed occupations, as a dielectric case: the
! heavy-element counterpart of `alas-epsilon-us-soc.in`.
!
! AlAs answers whether the augmentation terms of a spinor response are there at
! all -- the dipole is worth 0.108 and `int3` 3.6e-4 against a residual of
! 3.5e-5 -- and cannot answer whether their **spin structure** is right: the
! `fcoef` dressing of `dpqq_so` is worth 1.6e-6 there and the dressing of the
! `int3` tangent 4.2e-7, both below the residual, so the comparison against
! `ph.x` would read the same with either one deleted. Bismuth is the heaviest
! species with a committed fully-relativistic ultrasoft dataset, and the whole
! point of the cell is that the dressing should be resolvable on it.
!
! `occupations = 'fixed'`, where `bismuthene-soc-small.in` smears: an
! `epsilon_infinity` is not defined for a metal and both codes refuse one. The
! honeycomb is a quantum spin Hall insulator once spin-orbit coupling is on, so
! the gap is the coupling's own doing.
!
! The reference is regenerated with the vendored `ph.x`
! (`bismuthene-epsilon-us-soc.ph.in`) and committed as
! `reference.out.ph-bismuthene-epsilon-us-soc`.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 4, celldm(1) = 10.109950, celldm(3) = 1.978248,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 20.0, ecutrho = 160.0,
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.,
    starting_magnetization = 0.0,
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Bi 208.98 Bi.rel-pbe-dn-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 Bi 0.333333333333 0.666666666667 0.0
 Bi 0.666666666667 0.333333333333 0.0
K_POINTS AUTOMATIC
 4 4 1 0 0 0
